<section class="three-tool-knowledge" id="nisab-deep-guide" aria-labelledby="nisab-guide-heading">
 <h2 id="nisab-guide-heading">Nisab calculator: gold versus silver thresholds, hawl and complete guide</h2>
 <p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Nisab is the minimum wealth reference used when assessing annual Zakat. Using the widely followed UK weights of <strong>87.48g of gold</strong> and <strong>612.36g of silver</strong>, multiply each by its verified per-gram price to get two different money benchmarks. Which benchmark and which holdings apply must be checked with qualified guidance. <a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1405" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1405</a> reports the silver threshold in the classical unit of awaq.</p>
 <nav class="three-tool-nav" aria-label="Nisab guide sections"><a href="#nisab-meaning">Meaning</a><a href="#nisab-metals">Gold vs silver</a><a href="#nisab-hawl">Hawl</a><a href="#nisab-example">Worked example</a><a href="#nisab-steps">How to</a><a href="#nisab-faqs">FAQs</a><a href="#nisab-sources">Sources</a></nav>
 <div class="three-tool-facts"><div><span>Gold reference</span><strong>87.48g</strong><p>24k pure gold benchmark</p></div><div><span>Silver reference</span><strong>612.36g</strong><p>Fine silver benchmark</p></div><div><span>Conditional rate</span><strong>2.5%</strong><p>When requirements are met</p></div></div>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="nisab-meaning"><h3>What is Nisab in Islam?</h3>
 <p>Nisab identifies a minimum amount of qualifying wealth. It is traditionally measured by gold and silver weights and is one condition used to decide whether regular Zakat is due. A person may own money, investment assets, business stock or precious metals: what matters to the assessment is the set of holdings and liabilities recognised under their applicable guidance, not only what they have in one bank account.</p>
 <p><a href="https://sunnah.com/bukhari:1405" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih al-Bukhari 1405</a> contains the Prophetic report of no Zakat below five awaq of silver. Modern numerical gram conversions are derived from classical measures; some scholarly references use slightly different figures. We identify and consistently apply the <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/zakat/nisab/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">87.48g / 612.36g convention used by Islamic Relief UK</a>.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="nisab-metals"><h3>Gold Nisab versus silver Nisab: why there are two values</h3>
 <p>Gold and silver have significantly different present-day market prices. The same person's qualifying wealth can exceed one threshold and remain below the other, which makes the scholarly reference choice material. Some authorities recommend the silver benchmark because it can increase the population who give Zakat; others rely on the gold benchmark in specified circumstances. The <a href="https://www.islamic-relief.org.uk/giving/islamic-giving/zakat/nisab/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Islamic Relief UK discussion</a> provides one contemporary interpretation, not a universal consensus.</p>
 <p>The calculator displays both results at once. It does <strong>not</strong> choose whichever is easier to exceed, nor does it suggest that a user should pay twice. If the same entered net wealth passes one threshold but not the other, that is a comparison to discuss with your chosen religious authority.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="nisab-hawl"><h3>What is hawl, and does reaching Nisab today make Zakat due?</h3>
 <p><em>Hawl</em> is an Islamic lunar year relevant to Zakat on many forms of wealth; it generally lasts 354 or 355 days, rather than a Gregorian year. The determination of its starting point, treatment of wealth that fluctuates below a threshold, and income acquired during the year can differ by school and circumstances. Consequently, a successful threshold comparison in the browser does not settle a person's obligation.</p>
 <p>Use the <a href="/tools/zakat-anniversary-calculator/">Zakat anniversary calculator</a> for a planning date if needed, but treat it as a reminder rather than an official decision. Full Zakat calculations should include all applicable assets and only permissible deductions; see our <a href="/tools/zakat-calculator/">Zakat Calculator</a>.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="nisab-steps"><h3>How to calculate Nisab from market prices step by step</h3>
 <ol class="three-tool-steps"><li>Check a credible source for the current price per gram of <strong>24k pure gold</strong> and <strong>silver</strong> on the intended assessment date.</li><li>Enter both prices in the same currency. The selector only controls the label and clears prior figures when changed; no live exchange rates are applied.</li><li>Multiply gold price by <strong>87.48</strong> and silver price by <strong>612.36</strong>. The outputs are alternative threshold values.</li><li>Optionally enter independently identified qualifying wealth and those liabilities permitted by your scholarly approach. This step is not necessary merely to see thresholds.</li><li>Compare the net wealth to each result and review which benchmark is appropriate with qualified guidance.</li><li>Check hawl and other rules before deciding whether any 2.5% Zakat estimate is payable.</li></ol></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="nisab-example"><h3>Worked example: a household's qualifying wealth between the two thresholds</h3>
 <div class="three-tool-example"><p><strong>Hypothetical metal prices:</strong> 24k gold at <strong>£80 per gram</strong> gives <strong>87.48 × £80 = £6,998.40</strong>. Silver at <strong>£1.20 per gram</strong> gives <strong>612.36 × £1.20 = £734.83</strong> when rounded to pennies.</p><p>Suppose qualifying assets are £2,000 and appropriate deductions are £200, leaving <strong>£1,800</strong>. This exceeds the illustrative silver threshold but not the gold threshold. If the silver reference is the proper one and the other requirements are met, 2.5% of £1,800 is <strong>£45</strong>. If gold is the applicable benchmark, the calculator shows a below-threshold result. This does not resolve which rule applies.</p></div>
 <p>The market examples are deliberately invented, not 2026 quotations or an up-to-date UK Nisab figure. Market values change, so rerun the comparison with a rate verified for your assessment date.</p></section>
 <section class="three-tool-section" id="nisab-deductions"><h3>What belongs in net qualifying wealth?</h3>
 <p>Cash, savings, eligible gold and silver, qualifying business goods, appropriate investments and money recoverably owed to you may need to be considered. Personal necessities and non-trading household goods can be different. The debt allowance also varies; do not indiscriminately subtract your entire future mortgage or long-term repayment schedule.</p>
 <div class="three-tool-comparison"><div><h4>Assets first</h4><p>Count relevant holdings once and use an assessment-date valuation.</p></div><div><h4>Permitted deductions</h4><p>Check which current liabilities the chosen method allows rather than subtracting every obligation.</p></div><div><h4>Compare thresholds</h4><p>See both metal bases, not an automatically selected cheapest benchmark.</p></div><div><h4>Confirm hawl</h4><p>Threshold arithmetic cannot confirm the required lunar-year conditions.</p></div></div></section>
